{"id": "sdqa0077", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 5.4, "n_chunks": 6, "n_concurrent": 6, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.0989, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": "Speed describes only how fast an object is moving, whereas velocity gives both how fast it is and in which direction the object is moving", "query": "What is the difference between speed and velocity?", "transcript": "What is the difference between speed and velocity?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Speed and velocity are both ways to describe how fast something is moving, but they are not exactly the same thing. Here's the difference between speed and velocity in simple terms for kids who want to learn physics early on and have a strong curiosity about science! Speed tells you how fast something is going, but it doesn't care about the direction. For example, if you're riding your bike at 10 kilometers per hour, that's your speed. You could be going straight ahead, or you could be turning left or right. Your speed stays the same as long as you keep pedaling at the same pace. Velocity tells you how fast something is going and in what direction. So using the bike example again, if you're riding your bike at 10 kilometers per hour straight ahead, that's your velocity. But if you turn around and start going backward while still keeping up the same pedal speed, your speed might stay the same, but your velocity would change because you're now going in the opposite direction. In short, speed is just about how fast, while velocity is about how fast and which way! This helps us understand motion better, especially when we want to know not just whether something is moving quickly or slowly, but also where it's headed.", "answer_ms": 8845}
