{"id": "sdqa0152", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 2.8, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2451, "eot_read_ms": 27, "reference_answer": "in the May 1971 Creem, Saunders wrote, \"Sir Lord Baltimore seems to have down pat most all the best heavy metal tricks in the book\".[99] Creem critic Lester Bangs is credited with popularizing the term via his early 1970s essays on bands such as Led Zeppelin and Black Sabbath", "query": "Why is heavy metal called heavy metal?", "transcript": "Why is heavy metal called heavy metal?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The name \"heavy metal\" comes from the music's loud, intense sound. The music uses heavy distortion on electric guitars, strong drum beats, and powerful vocals. It often talks about deep or dark topics like anger, pain, or rebellion. So, it's called \"heavy\" because it feels heavy and strong to listen to.", "answer_ms": 2033}
